58
00:02:54,910 --> 00:02:57,470
So first, we're going
to talk about the

59
00:02:57,470 --> 00:03:00,170
normal endothelium. So
as you guys all know,

60
00:03:00,370 --> 00:03:04,670
it's a single layer of cells
that makes up the wall of

61
00:03:04,670 --> 00:03:07,010
the blood vessel. And under
normal conditions, when

62
00:03:07,010 --> 00:03:09,450
there's no injury, it has
many properties that make it

63
00:03:09,450 --> 00:03:12,890
antithrombotic. So there are
different effector molecules

64
00:03:12,890 --> 00:03:15,330
that are released from the
endothelium. Some of these

65
00:03:15,330 --> 00:03:19,110
are nitric oxide, prostacyclin,
and diphosphatases.

66
00:03:19,110 --> 00:03:21,790
These prevent platelets
from sticking to the vessel

67
00:03:21,790 --> 00:03:24,130
wall and just allow them
to pass through smoothly.

68
00:03:24,710 --> 00:03:27,370
There are also membrane
-bound molecules

69
00:03:27,370 --> 00:03:29,770
present on the
endothelium that inhibit

70
00:03:29,770 --> 00:03:31,970
clotting factors
from being activated.

71
00:03:32,330 --> 00:03:35,370
Heparin-like molecules
bind antithrombin-3.

72
00:03:35,510 --> 00:03:38,690
There is thrombomodulin,
which binds thrombin

73
00:03:38,690 --> 00:03:41,310
and protein C. Both
